<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Stavelot-Malmedy</h1><p class="st-meta">651 CE – 1795 CE (approx.) · capital Stavelot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny ecclesiastical principality of the Ardennes, ruled for over a thousand years by the abbots of twin monasteries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 648 to 651 the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remaclus">Remaclus</a> founded twin abbeys at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot">Stavelot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> forest, on land granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-iii">Sigebert III of Austrasia</a>. The two houses shared a single abbot, and the community became one of the leading monasteries of the region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> the abbots became princes with a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>, ruling a small territory between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">Duchy of Luxembourg</a>. Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wibald">Wibald</a> in the 12th century was an adviser to emperors, and the abbey's workshops produced masterpieces of Mosan goldsmith work such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot-triptych">Stavelot Triptych</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">Revolutionary France</a> annexed the principality in 1795 and dissolved the abbeys. In 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot">Stavelot</a> went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Malmedy was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in 1920, reuniting the two towns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-stavelot-malmedy.jpg" alt="Principality of Stavelot-Malmedy" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Principality_of_Stavelot-Malmedy_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>651 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stavelot</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Remaclus, Wibald</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Walloon, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Stavelot-Malmedy, Imperial Abbey of Stavelot</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1223&amp;focus=principality-of-stavelot-malmedy" aria-label="Principality of Stavelot-Malmedy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4815 427 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Stavelot-Malmedy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5135 683L5054 728M5135 683L5229 694M5135 683L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5135 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5135 683h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Stavelot. <a href="/atlas?year=1223&amp;focus=principality-of-stavelot-malmedy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

